2026-07-27 22:07:04,619 - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/embeddings', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-d80a2a83-70d9-417d-b6f6-74b66b964248', 'post_parser': <function Embeddings.create.<locals>.parser at 0x7fe7c77f3f60>, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'input': "CISA warns that multiple vulnerabilities in SharePoint are under exploitation\n\n“We see threat actors beginning exploitation of disclosed vulnerabilities, including ones deduced from vendor patch notes, within 24 to 72 hours of release. Security researchers warned that additional flaws are being chained together and a patch will not be available until August. The existing threat activity against SharePoint instances appears to be targeted rather than a broad campaign, according to VulnCheck. VulnCheck researchers pointed out that a remote code execution vulnerability in SharePoint tracked as CVE-2026-58644 was patched in June, but was inadvertently left off Microsoft’s June patch update cycle. “So, all in all, we're seeing a regular stream of new SharePoint vulnerabilities that are seeing a mix of pre-disclosure zero-day exploitation and longer-tail n-day exploitation,” said Caitlin Condon, VP of security research at VulnCheck. Condon noted the 2025 campaign, known as ToolShell, led to a mass exploitation campaign. CISA urged security teams to make sure Microsoft’s Antimalware Scan Interface is enabled for each SharePoint web application. Microsoft has provided guidance on how to integrate AMSI with SharePoint. Security teams should rotate internet information services machine keys, but also check for any intrusion artifacts, including machine key harvesters, according to CISA. Users should avoid exposing SharePoint directly to the internet and also block external access to SharePoint Central Administration, CISA said. CISA provided a link to a SharePoint hardening guide from Microsoft. Editor’s note: Updates with comments from VulnCheck.", 'model': 'text-embedding-3-small', 'encoding_format': 'base64'}}
